Runic: L-System & Bitmap Graphics Engine
Overview
This project implements a procedural graphics generator in C, capable of rendering L-Systems, handling Bitmap (BDF) fonts, and performing basic steganography. The architecture is strictly modular, separating low-level data management from high-level application logic.

Architecture
utils.h (The Foundation)
This file defines the core data structures (image, font, program_state) and handles all memory management. It implements two specific data structures chosen for their algorithmic efficiency in this context:

Queue (FIFO): Used for input parsing. A queue is essential here to process command-line tokens (commands and arguments) sequentially, ensuring execution happens in the exact order the user intends.

Stack (LIFO): Used for state management. This is the backbone of the Undo/Redo system. Since the "Undo" action naturally requires reverting to the most recent previous state, a Last-In-First-Out structure is the optimal choice.

runic.c (The Engine)
This file contains the main loop and application logic. It links the tools from utils to execute complex tasks:

Turtle Graphics: Implements the L-System expansion. It uses Bresenham's line algorithm for drawing. Crucially, it uses a local stack to handle branching (the [ and ] characters), allowing the "turtle" to save and restore its position and angle during fractal generation.

State Control: Before any destructive operation (drawing, loading files), a deep copy of the current program_state is pushed onto the global undo stack, ensuring robust history navigation.

This separation of concerns keeps runic.c clean and focused on logic, while utils.h abstracts away the complexity of memory allocation and pointer manipulation.

Technical Deep Dive
Procedural Generation: L-System Derivation
The core of the procedural generation lies in the app_der_to_buff function. To handle string expansion efficiently, the engine employs a double-buffering technique. During each iteration of the L-System derivation, the current string (Axiom) is read and expanded into a secondary buffer. This avoids the overhead of constant reallocations and the complexity of in-place string modification. Once the derivation is complete, the buffers are swapped, allowing the engine to handle thousands of symbols without significant performance degradation.

Graphics Rendering: The Rasterization Layer
The drawing logic operates on a custom image structure, which maps pixels in a 2D array. A dedicated set_pixel_safe function acts as a guardrail, preventing out-of-bounds memory access (segmentation faults) and handling the coordinate system transformation. While standard screen coordinates often place (0,0) at the top-left, Runic treats the bottom-left as the origin to align with traditional mathematical Cartesian logic.

Lines are drawn using Bresenham’s Algorithm, which is implemented to use only integer arithmetic (additions and bit-shifts). This ensures that the turtle graphics remain fast and precise even when generating highly complex fractal structures like the Dragon Curve or Sierpinski triangles.

Typography: BDF Font Processing
The engine includes a robust parser for Bitmap Distribution Format (BDF) files. Unlike simple fixed-width fonts, Runic supports variable-width glyphs by respecting the DWIDTH (device width) and BBX (bounding box) metrics. Each character is rendered bit-by-bit from its hexadecimal representation, allowing for high-fidelity text overlays on generated images.

Steganography and Data Integrity
Beyond graphics, Runic features a steganographic module. The BITCHECK command implements a Least Significant Bit (LSB) analysis across the RGB channels. It is designed to detect and "repair" data corruption by scanning for specific bit-flip patterns (e.g., 0010 or 1101). This demonstrates the engine's capability to treat image data as a generic bit-stream for hidden message storage and verification.

Memory Philosophy
The project strictly adheres to the Linux Kernel coding style, emphasizing a "no global variables" policy. Every function receives its required context through pointers, ensuring that the program_state remains isolated and thread-safe. Extensive "deep copy" logic ensures that when a state is pushed to the Undo stack, every nested pointer (including pixel arrays and font glyphs) is duplicated, preventing pointer dangling and memory leaks during complex operations.